<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constantine Phaulkon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1647">1647</a>–<a href="/years/1688">1688</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Constantine Phaulkon was a Greek adventurer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kefalonia">Cephalonia</a> who came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">English East India Company</a> and became the chief minister of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narai">Narai</a> in the 1680s. He promoted close ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, which sent embassies and troops, arousing the hostility of Siamese nobles. As Narai lay dying in 1688 he was arrested and executed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phetracha">Phetracha</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantine-phaulkon.jpg" alt="Constantine Phaulkon" width="320" height="568" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pierre-Joseph d'Orléans (1641-1698) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Constantine_Phaulkon_Portrait_Engraving.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under King Narai (r. 1656–1688) the court engaged actively with Europe: the Greek adventurer <b>Constantine Phaulkon</b> rose to high office, a French embassy under the Chevalier de Chaumont arrived in 1685, and the Siamese envoy Kosa Pan was received by Louis XIV at Versailles in 1686.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Offices were mostly inherited, but the king could raise new men, and foreigners often rose high: the Greek adventurer <b>Constantine Phaulkon</b> became Narai’s prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ayutthaya">Social Hierarchy of the Ayutthaya Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Assos Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Lopburi, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.38,20.53&amp;year=1673" aria-label="Assos Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5060 785 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Constantine Phaulkon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5510 1055h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5510 1055h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.38,20.53&amp;year=1673">What was here in 1673? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
